I own all Elemental games, and while this is by far the best of them, it never grew on me. I love 4X games, and probably own most of the known ones, but this one feels like it's lacking something.
**A few points:**
* tons of content in this game: items, loot, weapons, spells, there's a lot of them;
* mob variety: a lot of variety, and they scale well among all levels and all of them have a little flavor description;
* total lack of empire management: nothing at all; no "empire plan" or "social policies" or "virtues" or anything similar to adapt your empire;
* diplomacy is rather dull and almost useless, because every AI will eventually hate you and want to kill you because of such modifiers as "we want to establish order to the world" which makes them want to destroy you even if they are your ally. You cannot play peacefully in this game, you will be rolled over if you don't maintain a huge army at all times;
* quests on the map: similar to Endless Legend, only way more boring and uninteresting. They have huge amount of uninteresting text. The Endless Legend ones seem a lot more compelling and they tie in to the lore of the game, not here;
* combat is dull: there's a *swarm* mechanic, where all your adjacent units will attack an enemy, but that's about the only twist there is to it. The combat itself is not bad, and spells are actually interesting and strategic, but the presentation of the combat is dull and all units looking almost the same doesn't help either;
* performance: surprisingly poor for the look of the game. The engine is much more optimized then it was before, I have reported the issue a long time ago and Brad, the CEO of Stardock took personal interest in fixing this, and they did, but still I get the same FPS as in Farcry 3 so this might be an issue for some people;
* turn wait time: almost inexistent because the AI things while you take your turn and just executes the moves when you press the button;
* late game is a nightmare: there are no governors for the cities and destroying the cities can only be done after 5 turns after it has been conquered. Also, destroying a city, leaves "fertile" ground where the AI could build another one;
* developing cities is honestly as dull as it is in Age of Wonders 3: you choose one of three specializations: Science, Military/Production or Growth/Money and from then on you just build the specialized buildings in the same order, you can just queue them for 100 turns, it makes no difference;
* race differentiation: very little; they all look the same, except for some DLC ones, and the only difference is in bonus stats and sometimes a unique unit or building. Nothing even remotely close to the extent of Endless Legend;
That being said, I cannot recommend this game, because there's Endless Legend and Age of Wonders 3. They're both better games for different reasons: Endless Legend for civilization building and economy and trade and diplomacy, and Age of Wonders 3 for the superb combat and unit variety and that sense of fantasy adventure.
